1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,063
On the ninth of March 2023, customers pulled more

2
00:00:04,063 --> 00:00:08,136
than 40 billion dollars out of Silicon Valley Bank.

3
00:00:08,136 --> 00:00:09,414
In a single day.

4
00:00:10,083 --> 00:00:16,050
The next morning, its managers expected more than 100 billion dollars to follow.

5
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:20,451
Together, that was roughly 85 per cent of all its deposits.

6
00:00:21,049 --> 00:00:26,007
On the tenth of March, California's financial regulator closed it.

7
00:00:26,007 --> 00:00:30,890
A banking group with about 212 billion dollars of assets had lost

8
00:00:30,890 --> 00:00:35,246
its bank, two days after announcing a plan to raise money.

9
00:00:35,916 --> 00:00:38,014
This was not a small lender.

10
00:00:38,014 --> 00:00:40,712
It had tripled in size in two years.

11
00:00:40,712 --> 00:00:44,684
And about 94 per cent of its deposits were uninsured.

12
00:00:45,283 --> 00:00:49,152
So how does a group that size run out of money in two days?

13
00:00:49,152 --> 00:00:50,595
And who saw it coming?

14
00:00:51,549 --> 00:00:56,448
The newest official review says supervisors knew, or should have

15
00:00:56,448 --> 00:01:00,351
known, about its weaknesses as early as March 2022.

16
00:01:00,351 --> 00:01:02,417
A full year before the run.

17
00:01:03,083 --> 00:01:08,651
This is the story of Silicon Valley Bank, told from the regulators' own records.

18
00:01:09,616 --> 00:01:13,477
Silicon Valley Bank was founded in 1983,

19
00:01:13,477 --> 00:01:17,434
and was based in Santa Clara, California.

20
00:01:18,116 --> 00:01:22,959
At the end of its first year, it had about 18 million dollars of assets.

21
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:27,928
It built its business around one kind of customer: technology and

22
00:01:27,928 --> 00:01:33,317
life-sciences companies, many of them young start-ups backed by venture capital.

23
00:01:33,983 --> 00:01:35,918
That focus was its strength.

24
00:01:35,918 --> 00:01:39,995
But it also meant its customers had a great deal in common.

25
00:01:39,995 --> 00:01:45,247
The Federal Reserve later described the client base as heavily concentrated.

26
00:01:45,850 --> 00:01:48,459
Then the money poured in.

27
00:01:48,459 --> 00:01:53,259
Between 2019 and 2021, the group's assets grew

28
00:01:53,259 --> 00:01:57,329
from 71 billion dollars to 211 billion.

29
00:01:58,050 --> 00:02:03,147
From April 2020 to the end of 2021, its deposits

30
00:02:03,147 --> 00:02:07,821
grew at an annual rate of about 89 per cent.

31
00:02:08,516 --> 00:02:11,324
A bank cannot leave deposits sitting idle.

32
00:02:11,324 --> 00:02:14,199
It lends them out, or invests them, so that

33
00:02:14,199 --> 00:02:16,940
it earns more than it pays its customers.

34
00:02:17,616 --> 00:02:22,608
Silicon Valley Bank made an unusual choice about where to put its money.

35
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:30,724
By the end of 2022, securities made up 55 per cent of the group's total assets.

36
00:02:31,316 --> 00:02:36,605
At large banking organisations, the typical share was about 25 per cent.

37
00:02:36,605 --> 00:02:39,764
Silicon Valley Bank's was more than double.

38
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:44,902
And 78 per cent of those securities were classed as held to

39
00:02:44,902 --> 00:02:50,534
maturity: long-dated bonds the bank intended to keep until they paid out.

40
00:02:51,216 --> 00:02:53,559
Long-dated bonds have a weakness.

41
00:02:53,559 --> 00:02:56,754
When interest rates rise, new bonds pay more,

42
00:02:56,754 --> 00:02:59,593
so older bonds that pay less lose value.

43
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:06,002
As interest rates rose in 2022, the Federal Reserve's review found the

44
00:03:06,002 --> 00:03:11,837
group saw a rapid increase in unrealized losses on its bond portfolios.

45
00:03:12,516 --> 00:03:16,596
An unrealized loss only becomes real if you have to sell.

46
00:03:16,596 --> 00:03:20,175
As long as depositors stayed, the bank could wait.

47
00:03:20,783 --> 00:03:23,441
There was also an accounting detail.

48
00:03:23,441 --> 00:03:28,463
The review notes that if unrealized losses on its available-for-sale

49
00:03:28,463 --> 00:03:32,967
bonds had been counted, the group's regulatory capital at the

50
00:03:32,967 --> 00:03:36,955
end of 2022 would have been 1.9 billion dollars lower.

51
00:03:37,716 --> 00:03:41,204
But the bank also changed how it measured the danger.

52
00:03:41,204 --> 00:03:44,494
The review says it made counterintuitive modelling

53
00:03:44,494 --> 00:03:47,586
assumptions about how long deposits would stay.

54
00:03:47,586 --> 00:03:50,613
And it removed interest-rate hedges that would

55
00:03:50,613 --> 00:03:53,179
have protected it against rising rates.

56
00:03:53,916 --> 00:03:59,559
Meanwhile, the review found, senior managers' pay through 2022 was tied to

57
00:03:59,559 --> 00:04:05,125
short-term earnings and equity returns, and did not include risk metrics.

58
00:04:05,816 --> 00:04:11,913
The Fed's inspector general later summed up the pattern: management emphasised growth, and

59
00:04:11,913 --> 00:04:16,180
failed to put in place the controls needed to manage the risks.

60
00:04:16,850 --> 00:04:20,371
The losses were real, even if they were unrealized.

61
00:04:20,371 --> 00:04:24,099
The preliminary findings of a later independent review

62
00:04:24,099 --> 00:04:26,999
say that they exceeded the bank's capital.

63
00:04:27,683 --> 00:04:31,679
Silicon Valley Bank was supervised by the Federal Reserve,

64
00:04:31,679 --> 00:04:35,123
through the Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco.

65
00:04:35,783 --> 00:04:42,339
In 2021, it was moved into the Fed's portfolio for large banking organisations.

66
00:04:42,339 --> 00:04:48,647
The San Francisco Fed's team watching it grew from about eight people to 20.

67
00:04:49,250 --> 00:04:53,575
But the inspector general found that the supervisory approach had

68
00:04:53,575 --> 00:04:57,102
not evolved with the bank's growth, and that the move

69
00:04:57,102 --> 00:05:00,828
to the large-bank portfolio was not handled effectively.

70
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:03,844
Supervisors did find problems.

71
00:05:03,844 --> 00:05:08,280
When the bank failed, it had 31 open supervisory findings.

72
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:11,875
That was about triple the number at peer firms.

73
00:05:12,483 --> 00:05:16,415
Yet the inspector general found the Fed did not

74
00:05:16,415 --> 00:05:20,933
downgrade the bank's overall rating until August 2022.

75
00:05:21,616 --> 00:05:24,776
The Fed's own review, led by its Vice Chair for

76
00:05:24,776 --> 00:05:28,674
Supervision, Michael Barr, was blunt about the Fed itself.

77
00:05:29,350 --> 00:05:33,639
And when supervisors did find problems, the review said, they did not

78
00:05:33,639 --> 00:05:37,991
take sufficient steps to make sure the bank fixed them quickly enough.

79
00:05:38,650 --> 00:05:43,064
By early 2023, the bank had large losses on its bonds,

80
00:05:43,064 --> 00:05:48,869
a concentrated customer base, and almost all of its deposits uninsured.

81
00:05:48,869 --> 00:05:50,749
It only needed a spark.

82
00:05:51,783 --> 00:05:54,905
On the eighth of March 2023, the group

83
00:05:54,905 --> 00:05:58,357
announced a plan to shore up its finances.

84
00:05:59,050 --> 00:06:02,576
It had sold 21 billion dollars of securities,

85
00:06:02,576 --> 00:06:06,024
booking a 1.8 billion dollar loss after tax.

86
00:06:06,024 --> 00:06:10,412
It planned to increase its borrowing by 15 to 30 billion

87
00:06:10,412 --> 00:06:14,957
dollars, and to raise 2.25 billion dollars of new capital.

88
00:06:15,716 --> 00:06:20,661
The inspector general later found that management's ineffective public

89
00:06:20,661 --> 00:06:25,395
communications about that plan contributed to the deposit outflows.

90
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:30,405
Government deposit insurance covers 250,000 dollars per

91
00:06:30,405 --> 00:06:34,286
depositor, per bank, for each ownership category.

92
00:06:34,286 --> 00:06:36,978
Above that, there is no guarantee.

93
00:06:37,650 --> 00:06:42,342
At Silicon Valley Bank, most deposits sat above that line.

94
00:06:42,342 --> 00:06:47,034
The Fed's 2023 review described depositors who, fuelled by

95
00:06:47,034 --> 00:06:52,374
social media, withdrew uninsured deposits in a coordinated manner.

96
00:06:53,050 --> 00:06:57,487
On the ninth of March, more than 40 billion dollars left the bank.

97
00:06:58,449 --> 00:07:04,954
For comparison, the review noted that in 2008, Wachovia lost about 10 billion

98
00:07:04,954 --> 00:07:10,952
dollars over eight days, and Washington Mutual 19 billion over 16 days.

99
00:07:11,649 --> 00:07:14,769
And for the next day, management expected more

100
00:07:14,769 --> 00:07:18,024
than 100 billion dollars of further withdrawals.

101
00:07:18,683 --> 00:07:22,170
On the tenth of March, California's Department of

102
00:07:22,170 --> 00:07:26,654
Financial Protection and Innovation closed Silicon Valley Bank.

103
00:07:27,316 --> 00:07:29,693
Two days later, on the twelfth of

104
00:07:29,693 --> 00:07:33,367
March, state regulators also closed Signature Bank.

105
00:07:34,050 --> 00:07:37,691
That Sunday evening, the Treasury, the Federal Reserve

106
00:07:37,691 --> 00:07:40,254
and the FDIC issued a joint statement.

107
00:07:40,916 --> 00:07:44,083
Depositors, it said, would have access to all of

108
00:07:44,083 --> 00:07:47,580
their money starting Monday, the thirteenth of March.

109
00:07:47,580 --> 00:07:50,813
That included deposits above the insurance limit.

110
00:07:51,483 --> 00:07:55,900
And, the agencies said, no losses associated with the resolution

111
00:07:55,900 --> 00:07:59,627
of Silicon Valley Bank would be borne by the taxpayer.

112
00:08:00,283 --> 00:08:01,417
So who did lose?

113
00:08:01,417 --> 00:08:06,093
Shareholders and certain unsecured debtholders were not protected.

114
00:08:06,093 --> 00:08:08,219
Senior management was removed.

115
00:08:08,816 --> 00:08:11,623
On the seventeenth of March, the parent

116
00:08:11,623 --> 00:08:15,294
company, SVB Financial Group, filed for bankruptcy.

117
00:08:15,950 --> 00:08:19,695
Over that weekend and the days after, the FDIC

118
00:08:19,695 --> 00:08:23,278
ran the business as a temporary bridge bank.

119
00:08:23,278 --> 00:08:27,349
As of the tenth of March, it had about 167 billion

120
00:08:27,349 --> 00:08:32,235
dollars of assets and about 119 billion dollars of deposits.

121
00:08:32,983 --> 00:08:34,248
A buyer emerged.

122
00:08:34,248 --> 00:08:38,990
First Citizens bought about 72 billion dollars of the failed

123
00:08:38,990 --> 00:08:43,180
bank's assets, at a discount of 16.5 billion dollars.

124
00:08:43,180 --> 00:08:49,266
About 90 billion dollars of securities and other assets stayed with the FDIC.

125
00:08:49,950 --> 00:08:53,490
The 17 former branches reopened under the First

126
00:08:53,490 --> 00:08:56,879
Citizens name on the twenty-seventh of March.

127
00:08:56,879 --> 00:09:00,043
The FDIC estimated the cost to its Deposit

128
00:09:00,043 --> 00:09:03,282
Insurance Fund at about 20 billion dollars.

129
00:09:04,016 --> 00:09:07,400
Of the cost of the SVB and Signature failures,

130
00:09:07,400 --> 00:09:12,475
about 16.3 billion dollars came from protecting uninsured depositors.

131
00:09:12,475 --> 00:09:18,065
The FDIC decided that banks would pay it back, through a special assessment.

132
00:09:18,750 --> 00:09:22,039
Banking organisations with less than 5 billion

133
00:09:22,039 --> 00:09:24,614
dollars of assets would pay nothing.

134
00:09:24,614 --> 00:09:29,835
So the bill for protecting uninsured depositors went to the larger banks.

135
00:09:30,850 --> 00:09:36,144
In April 2023, the Federal Reserve published its own review, along

136
00:09:36,144 --> 00:09:42,161
with more than two dozen documents of confidential supervisory information.

137
00:09:42,850 --> 00:09:44,239
It named four causes.

138
00:09:44,239 --> 00:09:47,813
The board and management failed to manage their risks.

139
00:09:47,813 --> 00:09:51,586
Supervisors did not fully appreciate the vulnerabilities.

140
00:09:51,586 --> 00:09:53,703
They did not act quickly enough.

141
00:09:53,703 --> 00:09:58,270
And the Board's tailoring rules, together with a shift in supervisory

142
00:09:58,270 --> 00:10:02,837
policy, had reduced standards and promoted a less assertive approach.

143
00:10:03,516 --> 00:10:06,730
Michael Barr wrote that the Fed must strengthen its

144
00:10:06,730 --> 00:10:10,258
supervision and regulation based on what it had learned.

145
00:10:10,916 --> 00:10:14,343
In September 2026, a second account arrived.

146
00:10:14,343 --> 00:10:20,496
Fed Vice Chair for Supervision Michelle Bowman described the first, preliminary

147
00:10:20,496 --> 00:10:25,558
findings of an independent review by the Starling Advisory Group.

148
00:10:26,250 --> 00:10:30,842
It found that staff knew, or should have known, about the bank's

149
00:10:30,842 --> 00:10:35,650
vulnerabilities as early as March 2022, and did not take prompt and

150
00:10:35,650 --> 00:10:40,314
decisive action to get the bank to reduce its interest-rate risk.

151
00:10:41,050 --> 00:10:44,714
But on two points, it pointed in a different direction.

152
00:10:44,714 --> 00:10:49,378
It found the delays were not caused by the regulatory tailoring rules,

153
00:10:49,378 --> 00:10:53,975
or by any directive from the Fed's former Vice Chair for Supervision.

154
00:10:54,650 --> 00:10:56,759
Instead, it pointed to culture.

155
00:10:56,759 --> 00:11:00,841
Staff, it found, believed it was personally safer to take no

156
00:11:00,841 --> 00:11:04,992
action unless they were certain the action was exactly right.

157
00:11:05,650 --> 00:11:09,553
And on social media, it reached a different conclusion.

158
00:11:09,553 --> 00:11:13,526
An analysis for the review found that 96 per cent of the

159
00:11:13,526 --> 00:11:19,275
relevant online chatter appeared after the bank's failure was already inevitable.

160
00:11:19,950 --> 00:11:24,089
So the 2023 review said social media fuelled the run.

161
00:11:24,089 --> 00:11:27,995
The 2026 findings say social media did not trigger

162
00:11:27,995 --> 00:11:31,432
it, and found no evidence it accelerated it.

163
00:11:31,432 --> 00:11:33,619
One is the Fed's own review.

164
00:11:33,619 --> 00:11:38,540
The other is the preliminary findings of an independent review.

165
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:39,946
They do not agree.

166
00:11:41,083 --> 00:11:44,425
Bowman said the Fed had already issued a statement

167
00:11:44,425 --> 00:11:48,970
of supervisory operating principles, without waiting for the review.

168
00:11:48,970 --> 00:11:53,516
She also announced monthly escalation reports from examination teams

169
00:11:53,516 --> 00:11:56,992
to the heads of supervision and their Reserve Banks.

170
00:11:57,716 --> 00:12:00,298
So what does the record actually show?

171
00:12:00,883 --> 00:12:05,223
Set the arguments aside, and the documents agree on the basics.

172
00:12:05,223 --> 00:12:07,083
A bank that grew very fast.

173
00:12:07,083 --> 00:12:11,768
Deposits concentrated in one industry, almost all of them uninsured.

174
00:12:11,768 --> 00:12:15,695
A large bet on long-dated bonds, with the hedges removed.

175
00:12:15,695 --> 00:12:19,208
And warnings that were not acted on quickly enough.

176
00:12:19,816 --> 00:12:24,024
Our reading is that the run was the last step, not the cause.

177
00:12:24,024 --> 00:12:27,266
The losses that made the bank fragile were, the

178
00:12:27,266 --> 00:12:31,267
latest review says, visible to supervisors a year earlier.

179
00:12:31,949 --> 00:12:33,549
Three lessons stand out.

180
00:12:33,549 --> 00:12:37,750
For businesses: know how much of your cash is actually insured.

181
00:12:37,750 --> 00:12:41,549
For banks: depositors who think alike can leave together.

182
00:12:41,549 --> 00:12:45,816
For supervisors: finding a problem is not the same as fixing it.

183
00:12:46,416 --> 00:12:49,435
Silicon Valley Bank took 40 years to build.

184
00:12:49,435 --> 00:12:51,190
It took two days to fall.

185
00:12:51,190 --> 00:12:56,806
And the warning signs, the records show, were on file long before the run began.

186
00:12:57,949 --> 00:13:02,597
Next on Filed Under Failure: another collapse, from the public record.

187
00:13:02,597 --> 00:13:04,656
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

188
00:13:04,656 --> 00:13:08,573
Every source for this episode is listed in the description.
